\section*{Summary}
Cataclysmic variables are interacting binary systems in which a white dwarf accretes material from a companion. This report uses Gaia DR3 data to identify the optical counterpart of 1eRASS J052113.8--520257, estimate its distance, and examine how its optical brightness changes over time. We also search the G-band observations for a repeating signal. The best-supported counterpart is Gaia DR3 source 4772021167680733696, separated by 0.83834 arcsec from the eROSITA source-region centre (the candidate CSV's separate reference-position offset is 0.021174 arcsec). Its parallax gives a formal inverse-parallax distance of $539.50\pm7.13$ pc. The 39 valid G-band observations span 940.56 days and show a total magnitude range of 3.455 mag. The unweighted periodogram shows a prominent feature near 33.92 h, close to the 34-hour value reported in the literature. Its global false-alarm probability (FAP), however, is 0.257 and does not meet the adopted 1\% significance criterion. The available observations therefore do not establish a reliable orbital period.

\section*{Gaia observations and quality assessment}
The raw epoch-photometry file contains 40 G-band transit records. One record has non-finite time and photometric values and no CCD observations, so it cannot be used. The remaining 39 measurements agree with the corresponding entries in the derived G-band data. Among these valid observations, 29 carry Gaia's noisy-data flag and two carry the G-rejected flag; a third rejected flag belongs to the invalid record. The noisy-data flag is a warning to interpret a measurement with care, not an instruction to discard it. We therefore retain these observations and mark them in the figures. The two valid G-rejected measurements are left out of the primary period search, and we check separately whether including them changes the result.

The target is classified as a cataclysmic variable: a binary system in which a white dwarf draws material from a companion. The accretion flow can produce changing optical emission, although the broad physical processes in accreting white-dwarf systems are varied~\cite{pageshaw}. Gaia's G-band measurement combines the light from the unresolved system. These observations cannot separate the contributions of the white dwarf, companion and accretion flow, nor can they explain the cause of each bright or faint visit. For that reason, the analysis first describes the variability that is actually observed and then asks whether the data also support a stable repeating signal.

\section{Part B: Optical counterpart and distance}
\subsection{Identification and catalogue properties}
Of the three Gaia candidates found near the target position, source 4772021167680733696 is the closest by a wide margin. The candidate-match CSV reports a separation of 0.021174 arcsec from its reference position (RA $80.3076200^\circ$, Dec $-52.0497800^\circ$); this is distinct from the separation measured from the eROSITA source position. The eROSITA source-region centre is RA $80.307391613^\circ$, Dec $-52.049972719^\circ$ (ICRS), from the \texttt{SRC\_REG} entry and \texttt{RA\_OBJ}/\texttt{DEC\_OBJ} keywords in HDU 1 of \texttt{em01\_081141\_020\_SourceSpec\_00007\_c010.fits}. Relative to that position, the Gaia source is separated by 0.83834 arcsec. The other Gaia candidates are 4.047773 and 7.639982 arcsec from the CSV reference position. The selected source is also the only candidate with epoch photometry in the supplied data and has a well-measured parallax, with a signal-to-noise ratio of 75.64. Taken together, the positional match and the available astrometric and photometric information make it the most convincing optical counterpart among the candidates. The catalogue measurements are from Gaia DR3~\cite{gaiadr3}.

