The two separation values refer to different reference coordinates: the CSV reference position and the eROSITA FITS source-region centre. The latter is the relevant offset from the X-ray source position and remains small compared with the offsets of the other candidates. The Gaia candidate CSV does not provide RUWE. The value shown is the value attributed to the Gaia DR3 \texttt{gaiadr3.gaia\_source.ruwe} field for this source identifier in the existing report; no Gaia query output was present in the supplied report bundle, and a fresh archive query could not be completed in this environment. The parallax is measured precisely relative to its uncertainty. Proper motion describes how the source moves across the sky, while RUWE indicates how well a single-source astrometric model fits the observations. A value close to unity is consistent with a good astrometric fit, but it does not prove that the system contains only one star or reveal its binary orbital properties.

\subsection{Parallax distance}
For parallax $p$ in milliarcseconds, the inverse-parallax estimate is
\begin{equation}
d=\frac{1000}{p}\quad\mathrm{pc}.
\end{equation}
Using the measured parallax and its formal uncertainty gives
\begin{align*}
d &= \frac{1000}{1.853551218}=539.5049\ \mathrm{pc},\\
\sigma_d &\simeq \left|\frac{\partial d}{\partial p}\right|\sigma_p
=\frac{1000(0.024504205)}{(1.853551218)^2}=7.1323\ \mathrm{pc}.
\end{align*}
The resulting distance is $539.50\pm7.13$ pc, or about $1759.6\pm23.3$ light-years. This uncertainty comes from propagating the formal parallax error to first order. It does not include systematic effects such as the Gaia parallax zero point, and the inverse-parallax estimate is not a full Bayesian distance inference. Given the high parallax signal-to-noise ratio, it is a useful approximation for this source. The distance also provides the scale needed for a future luminosity estimate, although that would require accounting for extinction and applying an appropriate bolometric correction.

As an independent catalogue comparison, Maan et al. report a distance of $545\pm15$ pc for this source~\cite{maan2025}. This is consistent with the Gaia DR3 inverse-parallax estimate within the quoted uncertainties. The comparison supports the scale of the distance estimate, while remaining distinct from the calculation performed here.

\section{Part C: Gaia G-band light curve}
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=.84\linewidth]{gaia_G_band_lightcurve.png}
\caption{Gaia G-band Vega magnitude as a function of BJD$_{\rm TCB}$. The magnitude axis is inverted, so brighter measurements appear higher. Error bars are propagated from the flux uncertainties; noisy epochs are orange and G-rejected epochs are red. All 39 valid observations are shown without connecting lines.}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Sampling and observed variability}
The G-band magnitude ranges from 13.435 to 16.890, a total span of 3.455 mag. The 39 valid observations cover 940.558 days, or about 2.58 years. The largest gap between visits is 64.886 days. A few observations occur in pairs separated by roughly two hours, but many visits are weeks apart. The light curve therefore gives a set of snapshots of the source rather than a continuous record. Any short-lived event, or change that took place during a long gap, is not resolved by these data.

The source changes noticeably in brightness, with a few isolated bright measurements and other intervals where the variations are smaller. There is no obvious repeating shape in the light curve. Changes in the accretion flow of a cataclysmic variable can affect its optical emission, but the present observations are not enough to identify a particular accretion state or explain individual bright and faint points. Gaia's broad G band also combines light from the unresolved system, so it cannot separate the accretion disc, white dwarf and companion. The light curve clearly shows optical variability, but it does not by itself show that this variability is tied to the binary orbit.

\subsection{Photometric quality and interpretation}
We keep the 29 noisy-flagged measurements because this flag calls for caution; it does not mean that every such measurement is unusable. Removing all of them would leave very few observations and would also change the already uneven sampling. The two valid G-rejected measurements are shown separately and excluded from the main period search. When they are included in a check, the preferred unweighted period changes very little, but the FAP remains above the adopted significance threshold. Thus, the conclusion below does not hinge on the treatment of these two points.

The error bars show the formal measurement uncertainties, not the full range of the source's intrinsic variability. This matters because the brightness changes are much larger than the measurement errors alone would suggest. With so few visits, an isolated bright point cannot confidently be labelled an outburst or assigned a physical timescale. More frequent optical monitoring would be needed to follow such events and to tell persistent modulation apart from temporary changes in brightness.

\section{Part D: Lomb--Scargle period analysis}
\subsection{Method and search range}
The Gaia observations are unevenly spaced, so a method that can work directly with the measured times is needed. The Lomb--Scargle periodogram does this by testing sinusoidal models without first interpolating the data onto a regular time grid. We use the generalized form, with a floating mean and one sinusoidal harmonic~\cite{vanderplas2018,astropy2022}. At a trial frequency $f$, the fitted model is
\begin{equation}
m(t)=C+A\cos(2\pi f t)+B\sin(2\pi f t),\qquad P=\frac{1}{f},
\end{equation}
where $C$ is the fitted mean and $A$ and $B$ determine the sinusoidal amplitude and phase. The periodogram power measures how much the sinusoidal model improves the fit relative to a constant-brightness model. It therefore tests for a coherent sinusoidal component, rather than every possible form of variability that may occur in a cataclysmic variable.

For the main period search, each retained observation is given equal weight. The formal magnitude uncertainties derived from the Gaia flux errors range from 0.001261 to 0.057179 mag. These values describe the precision of individual measurements, but they do not necessarily capture the source's intrinsic brightness changes. Giving every epoch equal weight provides a useful way to avoid letting a small number of very precise measurements dominate this sparse time series. This choice is not automatically superior to a weighted analysis; it is the approach used for the principal result here, and its limitations are considered below.

The main search covers frequencies from 0.02 to 24 cycles per day, corresponding to periods from 50 days down to 1 hour. The frequency grid is sampled more finely than the inverse of the full observation baseline, so nearby features in the periodogram can be examined. Because the Gaia visits are irregular, a Nyquist limit based on uniform sampling is not appropriate. A fine grid, however, cannot fill the long gaps or compensate for the small number of measurements.

We estimate the global false-alarm probability using the Baluev approximation over the full frequency range. In practical terms, it estimates how often the adopted noise-only model would produce a peak at least this strong somewhere in the search. We use 1\% as the significance threshold. The FAP depends on the assumed model and noise behaviour; it should not be read as the probability that a particular physical period is wrong.

\subsection{Period-search result and significance}
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=.86\linewidth]{lomb_scargle_periodogram.png}
\caption{Unweighted Lomb--Scargle periodogram for the 37-observation primary sample. The prominent feature remains below the adopted 1\% global false-alarm significance threshold.}
\end{figure}

The most prominent feature in the unweighted periodogram corresponds to a period of 33.9223 hours (about 1.4134 days). Its power is 0.5261 and its global FAP is 0.257, well above both 0.05 and the adopted 0.01 threshold. The feature is therefore not statistically significant, and these observations do not justify identifying it as the system's orbital period. Including the two G-rejected measurements gives a very similar period, close to 33.92 hours, but the global FAP remains high at 0.171. The conclusion is the same in either case: no significant period is detected in this dataset.

A periodogram always has a most prominent peak, but that peak is not necessarily evidence of a real period. Here it does not pass the global significance test, and the irregular brightness changes are not well described by a single sinusoid. We therefore quote 33.92 hours as a candidate timescale for comparison, not as a measured orbital period.

\subsection{Comparison with published period and phase-folded light curve}
Maan et al. report a 34-hour Gaia photometric period for 1eRASS J052113.8--520257~\cite{maan2025}. The period found here is close to that value, which makes the comparison interesting. However, the agreement should be treated cautiously: the candidate in our analysis has a global FAP of 0.257 and does not meet the adopted significance criterion. We use the published period as an independent point of comparison, not as a value to impose on the present data.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=.80\linewidth]{phase_folded_lightcurve.png}
\caption{G-band observations folded at the strongest candidate period from the unweighted periodogram. G-rejected observations are identified separately. The observations do not trace a sufficiently coherent repeating profile to confirm an orbital period.}
\end{figure}

Each observation is assigned a phase using
\begin{equation}
\phi=\left(\frac{t-t_0}{P}\right)\bmod 1,
\end{equation}
where $t_0=2456933.355081900$ BJD$_{\rm TCB}$ is the reference epoch and $P=1.4134276951$ days is the candidate period from the unweighted analysis. Folding brings observations from different dates onto a common 0--1 phase interval, making it easier to see whether they follow a repeating pattern. In this case, the points remain scattered rather than tracing a clear profile. Since the periodogram peak is not significant, the folded curve is useful for inspection but does not confirm an orbital modulation.

\clearpage
\section*{Conclusion}
The Gaia DR3 source 4772021167680733696 is the best-supported optical counterpart among the candidates, and its parallax places it at a formal distance of $539.50\pm7.13$ pc. The 39 valid G-band observations show substantial but irregular changes in brightness over 2.58 years. The unweighted Lomb--Scargle analysis identifies a candidate timescale near 33.92 hours, close to the 34-hour value reported in the literature. However, the global FAP of 0.257 is above the adopted 1\% threshold, and the phase-folded observations do not form a clear repeating pattern. The Gaia data therefore establish the counterpart, distance and optical variability, but do not provide a statistically reliable orbital period.

